{% import 'chrome/macros.html.j2' as c %}
<section>
  <h2>What This Matrix Does Not Prove</h2>

  <p>The project's own rule is that a green matrix must never imply coverage it does not have.
  These are the boundaries of what is claimed above.</p>

  {{ c.warn('Playwright WebKit is not Safari', 'The "WebKit (Playwright)" column reports no Storage API at all, which is a property of Playwright’s browser fork, not of Safari. Real Safari has OPFS and more. Every Safari row in the target table is documentation, not a measurement — nothing here was run on Apple hardware, which requires safaridriver or a device cloud.') }}

  {{ c.warn('The mobile lanes are emulated, not real devices', 'CI runs a 3-engine by 3-profile matrix: chromium, firefox and webkit, each against desktop, a Pixel 5 profile and an iPhone 13 profile. Those profiles are Playwright viewport and user-agent emulation. They do not reproduce the constraints that actually decide mobile behaviour — JavaScript suspension on backgrounding, page-memory ceilings, and Safari’s seven-day storage eviction. "Shown" in the Mobile advisory column means the warning renders; it is not evidence the underlying download survives a real phone.') }}

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Detected is not wired.</strong> The Managed-folder and Staged tiers are probed
    and labelled but are not built as transfer paths. Reading the capability table as a list of
    working download modes would overstate the app by two-thirds.</li>
    <li><strong>Some rows vary by provider, not browser.</strong> Hidden-versions behaviour has
    R2-specific handling, archived detection depends on the provider returning a StorageClass,
    and multipart-orphan cleanup differs between B2, R2 and AWS. That is a separate axis from the
    browser one and is not what this matrix measures.</li>
    <li><strong>Connection capability is a third axis.</strong> The app also has a per-credential
    capability panel (can this key read, write, delete?). That is about IAM permissions, not the
    browser, and is deliberately kept out of these tables.</li>
    <li><strong>This is a snapshot.</strong> Every cell was read from source at commit
    <code>81ebc5a</code> (v1.44.3, both <code>main</code> and the branch tip) on 2026-08-02. It
    drifts the moment the code does — in particular, the day a second transfer tier is wired,
    the "Transfer actually used" column stops being uniform.</li>
  </ul>

  {{ c.note('If you read one thing off this report', 'Production is v1.44.3 (deployed 2026-08-02) and includes the download manager. It gives every browser the same browser-managed handoff and differentiates by browser in exactly one place — offering folder verification on Chromium. The passphrase vault is deployed as code but deliberately unreachable. Everything else Bucketer does works the same everywhere.') }}
</section>
